// Reference book: CLI Reference.
page cli { sites = [:reference, :wcl_skill]  title = "CLI Reference"
  h1 "CLI Reference"

  p "The `wcl` binary drives parsing, checking, evaluation, editing, formatting, the language server, and wdoc."

  h2 "wcl parse"

  p "Parse a file and print the resulting document tree (forcing full evaluation). `--profile` writes a call-tree profile as JSON to stderr."
  code bash {
    source = <<'BASH'
wcl parse site.wcl
BASH
  }

  h2 "wcl check"

  p "Parse and validate against the schema. Exit code `0` means valid, `1` a parse error, `2` a schema violation. System imports (`import <wdoc.wcl>`) resolve against the embedded wdoc library, so `wcl check` is the fast edit-loop checker for wdoc projects too — no full `wcl wdoc build` needed."
  code bash {
    source = <<'BASH'
wcl check site.wcl
BASH
  }

  h2 "wcl eval / wcl get"

  p "Resolve a dotted path from the document root and print the value. `--json` emits JSON; `--profile` writes a profile to stderr."
  code bash {
    source = <<'BASH'
wcl eval site.wcl service.web.port
wcl get  site.wcl name --json
BASH
  }

  h2 "wcl set"

  p "Update the field at a dotted path with a new WCL expression, following the import chain to the file that declares it. Quote shell-special values."
  code bash {
    source = <<'BASH'
wcl set site.wcl name '"alpha"'
wcl set site.wcl service.web.port 9090u32
wcl set site.wcl accent :gold
BASH
  }

  h2 "wcl fmt"

  p "Reformat to canonical form (comments and blank-line groupings preserved). `--in-place` overwrites; `--indent N` sets indent width; `--no-trailing-comma` drops the trailing comma after match arms."
  code bash {
    source = <<'BASH'
wcl fmt site.wcl                 # to stdout
wcl fmt site.wcl --in-place
wcl fmt site.wcl --indent 4
BASH
  }

  h2 "wcl diff"

  p "Compare two documents and print the changed entities and fields. The comparison is over the _evaluated_ document views (imports resolved), so a formatting-only edit yields an empty diff. Each top-level block is an entity keyed `kind:label`; a field edit inside an entity is reported by dotted path (`fields.due_date`), recursing into lists by index (`tags[2]`). An entity present on only one side is a single `added` / `removed`; a field going from unset (`none`) to a value reads as `added`."
  p "The default output is a re-parseable **WCL tree**: one `added` / `removed` / `modified` block per entity, each carrying the actual old/new values. Pass `--format json` for the flat array of change objects (also carrying `old` / `new` / `value`)."
  code bash {
    source = <<'BASH'
wcl diff old.wcl new.wcl
# modified "domain_entity:task" {
#   field "status" { kind = :changed  old = "draft"  new = "active" }
# }
# added "spec:impl_due_dates" {
#   value = { id: "impl_due_dates", title: "Due dates" }
# }

wcl diff old.wcl new.wcl --format json
# [ {"op":"modified","entity":"domain_entity:task","field":"status","kind":"changed","old":"draft","new":"active"}, … ]
BASH
  }
  p "Either side may be a `<rev>:<path>` git specifier — the file (and its imports) is read from that git revision, materialised via `git archive`."
  code bash {
    source = <<'BASH'
wcl diff HEAD~1:config.wcl config.wcl    # a committed version vs the working tree
wcl diff main:a.wcl feature:a.wcl        # across two branches
BASH
  }

  h2 "wcl init"

  p "Scaffold a new project folder from a WCL template. `<template>` is a built-in name (see `wcl init --list`), a user template installed under `$XDG_DATA_HOME/wcl/templates/<name>/template.wcl` (falling back to `~/.local/share/...`), or a path to a template `.wcl` file (or a folder holding a `template.wcl`); `[dest]` is the destination directory (default: the answered `name` property, falling back to the template name). Resolution prefers built-ins, then user templates, then disk paths."
  code bash {
    source = <<'BASH'
wcl init --list                                  # list built-in templates
wcl init minimal ./my-project                    # prompt for each property
wcl init minimal ./app -D name=app --defaults    # non-interactive, no prompts
wcl init minimal ./app --answers answers.json    # answers from a file (.wcl or .json)
wcl init ./my-template.wcl ./out                 # a template file of your own
BASH
  }
  p "A template declares `property` questions plus the `file` / `folder` blocks to generate. Each property's answer is resolved from, in order of precedence: a `-D key=value` flag, the `--answers` file, an interactive prompt (skipped under `--defaults` or when stdin is not a terminal), then the property's `default`. A property with no answer and no default is an error. The destination must be empty unless `--force` is given."
  p "Built-in templates: `minimal` (a single commented `main.wcl`), and three multi-folder wdoc projects — `page` (a website), `book` (a sidebar `toc`), and `presentation` (a slide `deck`). The multi-folder templates scaffold `main.wcl` plus `schema/`, `data/`, and `wdoc/` (each with its own `main.wcl`): the schema declares an `Entry` type and a typed top-level `entries` field, the data fills it, and the wdoc pages project that data into one generated page (or slide) per entry, cross-linked with a hand-written page."
  p "Inside a template, a file's contents reference answers through the `answer(\"name\")` builtin, using the interpolating heredoc form `$<<TAG` (a plain `<<TAG` is literal). Instance fields use `=`:"
  code wcl {
    source = <<'WCL'
import <scaffold.wcl>

property "name" {
  prompt  = "Project name"
  default = "my-project"
}

file "main.wcl" {
  content = $<<MAIN
// ${answer("name")}
// A new WCL document.
MAIN
}
WCL
  }

  h2 "wcl repl"

  p "An interactive read-eval-print loop. Pass a file to resolve identifiers against its top-level fields. `:quit` or EOF exits."
  code bash {
    source = <<'BASH'
wcl repl
wcl repl site.wcl
BASH
  }

  h2 "wcl lsp"

  p "Run the language server (diagnostics, formatting, symbols, go-to-definition, completion, hover, code actions). Defaults to stdio; `--tcp ADDR` listens on a socket and `--log FILE` writes trace logs."
  code bash {
    source = <<'BASH'
wcl lsp
wcl lsp --tcp 127.0.0.1:9257 --log /tmp/wcl-lsp.log
BASH
  }

  h2 "wcl wdoc"

  p "Build or serve a wdoc documentation site. `build` renders HTML to `--out`; `serve` runs a live-reloading dev server; `pdf` renders one PDF per site (`--page-size a4|letter`, default `a4`); `markdown` (alias `md`) renders a folder of `.md` files with diagrams as standalone `.svg` files, aimed at AI / text consumers; `skill` renders an agent / Claude skill folder (`SKILL.md` + `references/` + `scripts/` + `assets/`) from a `:ai_skill` site. `--site NAME` filters to one named site. `build` reports per-page progress on stderr when run interactively, and `--profile` writes a call-tree evaluation profile as JSON to stderr (like `wcl parse --profile`)."
  code bash {
    source = <<'BASH'
wcl wdoc build docs/main.wcl --out docs/_site
wcl wdoc serve docs/main.wcl --addr 127.0.0.1:8080
wcl wdoc pdf docs/main.wcl --out target/pdf --page-size letter
wcl wdoc markdown docs/main.wcl --out docs/_md
wcl wdoc skill docs/my-skill.wcl --out ./my-skill
BASH
  }
}
